// The one outcome NOTHING INSIDE THE BINARY CAN REPORT. `panic` is uncatchable, so the first test
// takes the whole process down: it writes a begin marker and never an end marker, and the second test
// never writes a begin marker at all. The only honest reading of that stream is "crashed" and "not
// observed"; what the second must never be read as is a PASS, since nothing watched it. The runner
// then re-queues the unobserved test into a FRESH process, where it is finally reported by whatever it
// really does there. Both halves are the subject: absence is classified as absence FIRST, and only an
// actual run turns it into a verdict.
//
// ⭐ **THE SECOND TEST ASSERTS RATHER THAN PRINTS, AND THAT IS WHAT PINS THE RE-RUN.** A test binary's
// stdout is captured and never read — attribution is the stderr marker stream — so a `print` here
// would prove nothing to anyone. Its `✓` in `expected.txt` is the evidence that its body really
// executed in the re-queued process; disable the re-queue and it reports DID NOT RUN instead.
//
// ⚠ **EDITING THIS FILE MOVES A LINE NUMBER THE EXPECTATION PINS.** The CRASHED block names the
// panicking line — `panic at ledger.test.maxon:N` — so inserting a line above the `panic` owes
// `expected.txt` a regeneration. The gate's report will say which line differs.
//
// ⚠ **THE PANIC MESSAGE IS A PLAIN LITERAL ON PURPOSE.** An interpolated one is a different lowering
// with its own contract, and pinning it here would make this case answer two questions at once.
typealias Cents = int(i64.min to i64.max)

function balanceAfter(owed Cents, paid Cents) returns Cents
	return owed - paid
end 'balanceAfter'

test 'a settled account that still owes takes the process down'
	panic("ledger: a settled account cannot still owe")
end 'a settled account that still owes takes the process down'

test 'an ordinary balance still runs after the crash'
	try Expect.equal(balanceAfter(500, paid: 200) as AssertedInt, expected: 300)
end 'an ordinary balance still runs after the crash'
